The bezel setting is particularly effective for emerald cuts. The metal wraps the horizontal edges of the stone, framing the rectangular silhouette and protecting the cropped corners. For radiant cuts, the pavé band setting adds sparkle around the center stone in a way that feels continuous rather than competing.

Prong count also differs by cut. Most emerald and radiant solitaires use four prongs — one at each corner. Six-prong settings exist but are less common for rectangular shapes.

Claw prongs (V-shaped) protect the trimmed corners of both cuts better than standard round prongs, which can slip off the corner facet over time. If longevity is a priority, ask your jeweler to confirm claw prongs at each corner.

